md(n,'The server never sees the payload. Client-side JavaScript reads','In DOM-based XSS, client-side JavaScript reads','DOM-based sources can originate from server-visible queries or persistedresponses.')
md(n,'Because the URL fragment (`#...`) is never sent to the server, server logs, WAFs and server-side encoding cannot see or stop this. The fix has to be in the client code.','A URL fragment is not included in the ordinary HTTP request target. In this example it reaches the sink entirely in the client, so server-side escaping of response values does not fix the flow. A page can explicitly transmit fragments later; server-supplied CSP can also limit execution. Fix the unsafe client sink.','Do not claimserverneverreceivesdataor cannotmitigateDOMXSS.')
md(n,'HTML escaping is correct for one context only.','HTML escaping helps in ordinary HTML text and safe quoted attributes, but is not a universal encoder.','Bodyandattributecontexts bothsupported, needattribute semantics.')
md(n,'**HTML body and quoted attributes**: HTML-escape.','**Ordinary HTML text and safe quoted attributes**: HTML-escape. Event handlers, style, srcdoc and URL values need their own restrictions; quoting alone does not make their semantics safe.','Unsafeattributecontextsnotmade safebyescaping.')
md(n,'XSS in these frameworks almost always comes from the **escape hatches**:','Important review points include these **escape hatches**, alongside unsafe URLs, template injection, dependencies and framework-specific behaviour:','Unsupportedprevalenceclaimremoved.')
md(n,'Research by Google found these policies are bypassable in the large majority of real deployments, often because an allowed domain hosts something an attacker can abuse (an old library or a JSONP endpoint).','Google’s2016study found widespread bypasses in its sampled policies, including allowed hosts serving exploitable libraries or JSONP. This is a historical sample, not a current universal deployment statistic.','Dateandscoperesearchresult.')
md(n,'The modern recommendation is a **strict, nonce-based policy**:','One recommended defence is a **strict nonce-based policy**; hash-based strict policies are another option. Below is a formatted policy value, joined with spaces in one Content-Security-Policy response header, not folded HTTP wire syntax:','Validheaderrepresentationandhashalternative.')
md(n,"Content-Security-Policy:\n  script-src 'nonce-r4nd0mB64'\n    'strict-dynamic';\n  object-src 'none';\n  base-uri 'none'","script-src 'nonce-r4nd0mB64'\n  'strict-dynamic';\nobject-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'",'Formattedpolicyvalue distinguishedfrom HTTPfield; example nonceplaceholder notproductionentropy.')
md(n,'The browser runs only scripts with the matching nonce. Injected markup doesn\'t know the nonce, so it is refused. Inline event handlers and `javascript:` URLs are blocked too.','In a supporting browser, matching nonces permit inline or external script elements. Under this policy, unapproved parser-inserted scripts, inline event handlers and javascript URLs are blocked; strict-dynamic also permits eligible non-parser-inserted external scripts. Trusted-code gadgets or nonce leakage can still defeat the intended boundary.', 'Not onlynoncedscripts; avoidabsoluteexploitprevention.')
md(n,"`'strict-dynamic'` lets a trusted script load further scripts, so bundlers and tag managers keep working.","`'strict-dynamic'` permits eligible non-parser-inserted external scripts and ignores host/scheme allowlists in supporting implementations. Dynamic inline code still needs applicable permission; loaders must not use attacker-controlled URLs.",'Nonparserexternal distinction; noarbitrarybundlerguarantee.')
